Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Part 1: Chemical computation of algebraic expressions (30 points)\
\>", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.821677185136738*^9, 3.821677194303912*^9}, {
   3.822442876224731*^9, 3.8224428767220078`*^9}, {3.822875233169005*^9, 
   3.82287523366609*^9}, {3.823471213491994*^9, 3.82347122334372*^9}, {
   3.8255126980713873`*^9, 3.82551269814635*^9}, {3.825512755144827*^9, 
   3.8255127593745623`*^9}, 3.825512822098194*^9, {3.825513050845139*^9, 
   3.825513054022419*^9}, {3.825547659973978*^9, 3.8255476617754602`*^9}, {
   3.8515661573700457`*^9, 3.8515661601438637`*^9}, {3.883096010820916*^9, 
   3.883096010898616*^9}, {3.914468846679985*^9, 3.914468850896946*^9}, {
   3.91450708987049*^9, 3.9145070902042217`*^9}, {3.980511420427431*^9, 
   3.980511420477139*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"5ff758e3-9590-4c93-bc44-\
d30d966bc4d1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The goals of this homework are for you to:\n\n(1) Get familiar with the CRN \
simulator package and the examples in DeterministicCRNsInBulk.nb.\n(2) Use \
the modules discussed in class for analog catalytic steady-state computation \
to build circuits.\n(3) Think about the difference between systematically \
generated circuits and hand-optimized ones.\n\nSo, your first task is to \
choose an analog function of interest, where \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]interest\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] means that it is insightful to consider for the \
purpose of this homework, and/or you like it.  Don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t choose \
something so trivial such that it makes this homework dumb.  Don\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t choose something so complex that doing this homework \
becomes tedious.  If your function is an algebraic expression, great!  If \
not, reconsider whether you want to instead choose an algebraic expression.  \
If not, then consider and explain a suitable approximation, as necessary.  \
Discuss issues regarding input variables being positive and subexpressions \
remaining positive.\n\nExample:  You might choose an algebraic expression \
that represents a solution to the quadratic formula, or a pair of expressions \
that represent both possible solutions.   A natural choice would be for the \
input variables would be positive coefficients  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["a", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "f554b025-fbc8-4028-bb4c-51e15024ff84"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["b", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "1d2b1a3f-9bbd-4a8a-a0fb-3676dbda7290"],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["c", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5ff98ba0-df8a-4291-919e-3b932583c155"],
 "  for the quadratic polynomial ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"a", " ", 
      SuperscriptBox["x", "2"]}], "-", 
     RowBox[{"b", " ", "x"}], " ", "+", "c"}], "=", "0", " "}], 
   TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->"039bd923-d626-4dbf-8fef-0eb9e7b5708f"],
 ",  and the output variables would be \n\n\t",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"[", 
     SubscriptBox["x", "1"], "]"}], "=", 
    FractionBox[
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"[", "b", "]"}], "+", 
      SqrtBox[
       RowBox[{
        SuperscriptBox[
         RowBox[{"[", "b", "]"}], "2"], "-", " ", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"4", " ", "[", "a", "]"}], " ", "[", "c", "]"}]}]]}], 
     RowBox[{"2", " ", "[", "a", "]"}]]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "6cae3c87-ba7c-4d98-bc12-76af50111f6f"],
 "\nand\n\t",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"[", 
     SubscriptBox["x", "2"], "]"}], "=", 
    FractionBox[
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"[", "b", "]"}], "-", 
      SqrtBox[
       RowBox[{
        SuperscriptBox[
         RowBox[{"[", "b", "]"}], "2"], "-", " ", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"4", " ", "[", "a", "]"}], " ", "[", "c", "]"}]}]]}], 
     RowBox[{"2", " ", "[", "a", "]"}]]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "beab1bc9-9232-4b87-8dca-5fb9dc791979"],
 "\n\nat least for inputs where these values are positive with no imaginary \
part.   Under certain circumstances, we would get negative subexpressions, so \
thinking about when that happens, depending on where you put your \
parentheses, would be part of the problem.\n\nYou many analyze this example \
if you wish, but maybe it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s a little complex and you can \
choose another interesting algebraic expression that\[CloseCurlyQuote]s just \
as insightful to consider.\n\nYour second task is to convert your analog \
function to a CRN using the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]plug-and-play\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] circuit of modular approach described in class, and \
discuss any issues that arise there for your specific function.  Example \
issues to consider: When will it be guaranteed to go to steady state?  Are \
there any \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]hidden\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] intermediate \
species that might go to infinity?  For what input variable values is it \
guaranteed to give the correct output value in the steady-state limit?   How \
long, roughly, will it take to \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]almost\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] reach steady state (and what do you interpret that \
to mean, precisely)?\n\nYour third task is to examine the redundancy of the \
systematic construction.  Can modules be combined, by hand, to use fewer \
intermediate species, or to use fewer reactions?  Is there a systematic way \
to optimize the circuit, in this sense of using fewer species and reactions?  \
Note that it would be nice if your choice of analog function was one that \
made this optimality question as interesting as possible.\n\n",
 StyleBox["Don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t go overboard.  Use as few plots as possible \
to back up your derivation.  Be concise with your explanation.  All plots \
must be labeled and have sufficient explanation for what they are showing.  \
As always, please calibrate your choices about optional tasks such that this \
homework doesn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t take more time than necessary -- you are \
responsible for maximizing your understanding and skill development as a \
higher priority than rote obedience to the instructions.",
  FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8182163004062233`*^9, 3.818216324675933*^9}, {
   3.818216399775941*^9, 3.818216412086306*^9}, {3.818217589800467*^9, 
