Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Part 2: Systematic construction of a CRN with chaotic dynamics (30 points)\
\>", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.821677185136738*^9, 3.821677194303912*^9}, {
   3.822442876224731*^9, 3.8224428767220078`*^9}, {3.822875233169005*^9, 
   3.82287523366609*^9}, {3.823471213491994*^9, 3.82347122334372*^9}, {
   3.8255126980713873`*^9, 3.82551269814635*^9}, {3.825512755144827*^9, 
   3.8255127593745623`*^9}, 3.825512822098194*^9, {3.825513050845139*^9, 
   3.825513054022419*^9}, {3.825547659973978*^9, 3.8255476617754602`*^9}, {
   3.825547804513661*^9, 3.825547806147595*^9}, {3.851817374490266*^9, 
   3.8518173745860662`*^9}, {3.883613516817148*^9, 3.8836135194780903`*^9}, {
   3.980511426048304*^9, 3.980511426087739*^9}, {3.980619508009068*^9, 
   3.980619508082399*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"8ee6a86b-3d69-476c-bd5c-\
8e3c12e5ea09"],

Cell["\<\
You will be constructing a dual-rail bulk CRN that simulates the Rossler \
attractor.  There are two approaches you could use: (1) construct the CRN by \
hand, following the principles discussed in class, or if you really want to \
challenge yourself, (2) write Mathematica code that symbolically analyzes a \
set of polynomial ODEs and automatically builds the necessary CRN reactions \
for any dynamical system.  Full credit for approach (1), extra karma points \
(doesn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t affect your grade) for those who want to do (2).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.756693279002679*^9, 3.756693287269182*^9}, {
   3.756694017223577*^9, 3.75669402496581*^9}, {3.819656369036775*^9, 
   3.819656541162455*^9}, {3.8196565832834053`*^9, 3.819656646281259*^9}, 
   3.825550959459614*^9},
 Background->RGBColor[
  1, 0.9, 0.8],ExpressionUUID->"102547df-6f30-49eb-9a58-7fff96f41036"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Your first task is to construct a chemical reaction network that closely \
approximates the chaotic R\[ODoubleDot]ssler attractor:                       \
                         \n\t           ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    FractionBox["dx", "dt"], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"-", "z"}], "-", "y"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "c148cb06-83ff-4454-99c1-cda385cce733"],
 " \n\t           ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    FractionBox["dy", "dt"], "=", 
    RowBox[{"x", "+", 
     RowBox[{"a", " ", "y"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e2b418d1-6772-43c1-9272-38ee3e134506"],
 "\n\t           ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    FractionBox["dz", "dt"], "=", 
    RowBox[{"b", "+", 
     RowBox[{"z", " ", 
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{"x", "-", "c"}], ")"}]}]}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "0d048782-8c69-4aa7-8dcb-9948284cbe1a"],
 "\nfor the parameters ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"a", "=", 
    RowBox[{"b", "=", "0.2"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "9180b9c9-41c0-4f6a-8e0b-36d04bf27629"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"c", "=", "5.7"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e4efa46a-a7a2-46a8-a586-0aa5c38e10c1"],
 ".  Using a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]dual rail\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
representation for analog values, your CRN should have formal species ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["X", "+"], ",", " ", 
    SubscriptBox["X", "-"], ",", " ", 
    SubscriptBox["Y", "+"], ",", " ", 
    SubscriptBox["Y", "-"], ",", " ", 
    SubscriptBox["Z", "+"], ","}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "804d46ed-b5e0-4a68-9bab-1e257245f5dd"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["Z", "-"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "6969ab97-fd93-48cb-b545-f124809679e5"],
 " that collectively have the desired dynamics, i.e. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"x", "(", "t", ")"}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"[", 
       SubscriptBox["X", "+"], "]"}], 
      RowBox[{"(", "t", ")"}]}], "-", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"[", 
       SubscriptBox["X", "-"], "]"}], 
      RowBox[{"(", "t", ")"}]}]}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "9f12fff3-e27c-4629-aa0c-3442f244b645"],
 ", etc.  For starters, you will have the reactions\n\t\t",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["X", "+"], "+", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["X", "-"], 
     OverscriptBox["\[LongRightArrow]", 
      SubscriptBox["k", "fast"]], " ", "\[EmptySet]"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"9ad98603-8d49-4ab5-94de-857fb75d3d6d"],
 "\n\t\t",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["Y", "+"], "+", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["Y", "-"], 
     OverscriptBox["\[LongRightArrow]", 
      SubscriptBox["k", "fast"]], " ", "\[EmptySet]"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"671fc0dc-50b7-4dce-af73-84c03bf2e4e2"],
 "\n\t\t",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["Y", "+"], "+", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["Y", "-"], 
     OverscriptBox["\[LongRightArrow]", 
      SubscriptBox["k", "fast"]], " ", "\[EmptySet]"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"ef0057de-5e7a-4050-800b-68d88d3c03b0"],
 "\nThen you will add reactions that ensure that each term of the R\
\[ODoubleDot]ssler ODEs is effectively implemented.  Explain clearly how and \
why each step works.\n\nIn addition to making an analytic argument for the \
correctness of your CRN, you should directly illustrate and confirm its \
behavior using numerical simulations. Specifically, you should numerically \
simulate both the R\[ODoubleDot]ssler equations as given and the full mass \
action ODE\[CloseCurlyQuote]s for your CRN that is designed to implement the \
R\[ODoubleDot]ssler dynamics.\n\nA solid presentation would explain why these \
are the reactions we need and why they are correct for doing the job.  In \
addition to making an analytic argument for the correctness of your CRN, a \
healthy self-skeptic would directly illustrate and confirm the CRN\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s behavior using numerical simulations -- for example \
numerically simulating both the R\[ODoubleDot]ssler equations as given and \
the full mass-action ODEs for your CRN that is designed to implement the R\
\[ODoubleDot]ssler dynamics, and understand how different aspects of the \
solution depend upon the rate parameters, such as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["k", "fast"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "1d0f2510-bfcd-4d2b-b3dc-75cb96848088"],
 ".\n\nPlot ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3a159d12-a010-486b-a129-580caa4b759a"],
 " vs ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "914f4dbf-26f9-4346-abea-5bbaf26c1e72"],
 " as well as a parametric plot of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"x", "(", "t", ")"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"y", "(", "t", ")"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"z", "(", "t", ")"}]}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e50c558d-d21b-4aa6-851c-cf6bc61f8b88"],
 " for both systems.  Also, to illustrate the nature of the representation, \
plot ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"[", 
    SubscriptBox["X", "+"], "]"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "9e6969fe-6eb9-4f8d-9100-be48c948beb3"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"[", 
    SubscriptBox["X", "-"], "]"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "d49fea79-1dfe-4ed0-a394-3c5b0ec5633d"],
 " vs ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["t", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "cd0745c9-6360-48b4-9635-16d2a76a1c85"],
 ". Describe how these plots depend on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["k", "fast"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "862b7552-d3e6-4ccd-ab42-d43c0998e72e"],
 ".\n\n",
 StyleBox["The usual caveats apply to this homework -- if you want to change \
it up to make it more interesting (as opposed to just easier) we welcome \
that.  If you think there are other ways to tell a compelling story about how \
your constructions works, rather than the specific tasks  and plots we \
suggest, please do it the way that feels right to you.   The main point is \
that a reader shouldn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t have big question marks in their \
head when they read your work, and also that you shouldn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t \
belabor points if you just go on and on to cover all bases.  Keep it short \
and to the point!  Maybe we\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll start taking off points for \
people who do too much...",
  FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
