Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Part 3: Engage the material (30 points)", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.821677185136738*^9, 3.821677194303912*^9}, {
   3.822442876224731*^9, 3.8224428767220078`*^9}, {3.822875233169005*^9, 
   3.82287523366609*^9}, {3.823471213491994*^9, 3.82347122334372*^9}, {
   3.8255126980713873`*^9, 3.82551269814635*^9}, {3.825512755144827*^9, 
   3.8255127593745623`*^9}, 3.825512822098194*^9, {3.825513050845139*^9, 
   3.825513054022419*^9}, {3.8821134678978443`*^9, 3.882113469235524*^9}, {
   3.945824358548774*^9, 3.945824363957974*^9}, {3.946553847581139*^9, 
   3.946553874967773*^9}, 3.9465962046894903`*^9, {3.978024506593217*^9, 
   3.9780245680692587`*^9}, {3.979001019906189*^9, 3.979001034425283*^9}, {
   3.979001828606295*^9, 3.979001880310771*^9}, {3.979591259431616*^9, 
   3.979591259521995*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"23d306d3-aab7-4d3d-8c80-\
6329f03bbbf5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "As usual, this is place where you assign yourself an exercise and work \
through it to understand something uniquely of interest to you.  Thus is it \
kind of against the intentions if you just take one of our sample questions \
below and try to address it.  We want you to be asking your own question, and \
the sample questions are primarily there to serve as examples of the kinds of \
things that might be natural to ask... but there are an infinity of things to \
ask, so you ought to be able to come up with something equally interesting.  \
In fact, you ought to be coming up with at least one question every minute \
while you listen to a lecture or read the class notes or Mathematica \
notebooks.  They are all valid, so long as you engage with them to the depth \
that roughly matches the Part 1 and Part 2 questions... and that your \
questions relate to the material and concepts of this class, rather than some \
other class.   \n\nYou can think of your work here as being spiritually \
aligned a humanities essay, intended to challenge you to engage with the \
material and find your own meaning and understanding of it.    The only hard \
requirements are that, regardless of what question you eventually tackle, you \
go through everything in DeterministicCRNsInBulk.nb and DynamicalSystems.nb \
and contemplate it before you decide what you will do for the homework.   \
Read the article first; then write your essay about it.  So to speak. \n\n\
Note that although the notebooks explicitly present a number of sometimes \
very concrete questions, we don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t particularly want you to \
do anything that we suggested.  You can take these questions as example meant \
to stimulate your imagination.   You can work on these questions if that\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s what you want, but we think you\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll get \
much more out of it if you pose your own.\n\nAsking questions, allowing \
yourself to be curious, testing your knowledge in both simple and challenging \
cases... but especially, asking your own questions as a way to actively \
ground your understanding... is of such great importance that I really hope \
you\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll work hard to practice asking and following up on your \
ask.\n\nA good \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]essay\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] will \
clearly state a question and why you are interested in it, see how far you \
can go toward answering it, assess how rigorous your answer is in terms of \
whether there are exceptions, caveats, approximations, preconditions, etc., \
and comment on what it means to you.   A really good \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]essay\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] will have a punchline; \
it will convince the reader of something.  A good \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]effort\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] will perhaps have less \
clear of a question or questions, but will help ",
 StyleBox["you ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "understand the material by testing and trying things, even if it might not \
be so interesting to anyone else -- and that\[CloseCurlyQuote]s OK too.  \
Often the best questions are the simplest, the ones you think \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]nobody will be impressed if I understand that\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] -- but where understanding it well is actually the \
most valuable thing you can do.\n\nIn your write-up, clearly state your \
question related to programming computation and/or dynamics in CRNs, see how \
far you can go toward answering it, and assess how rigorous your answer is in \
terms of whether there are exceptions, caveats, approximations, \
preconditions, etc.\n\nBottom line: as with previous homework assignments, we \
are encouraging you to go in whatever directions compel or inspire you.  And \
we hope that will not be just copy-pasting one of the sample questions we \
posed in the notebooks!\n\nEnjoy!"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8182163004062233`*^9, 3.818216324675933*^9}, {
