Cell[TextData[{
 "Here are some sample questions about analog catalytic stead-state \
computation by CRNs.  These are too large-scale for most students, so don\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t that that as the template here -- we recommend asking \
simpler, more concrete questions. \n\nCan any algebraic expression that has \
positive output for all positive input values be written in a form such that \
all subexpressions are always positive?\n\nCan any algebraic expression be \
implemented by CRNs that use reactions with at most two reactants and at most \
two products?\n\nFor a different I/O convention where a real-valued variable \
is represented by the logarithm of its concentration, what analog function \
modules can we implement?\n\nFor a different I/O convention where all species \
concentrations must remain less than 1 at all times (e.g. they can\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t diverge to infinity) how does this restrict what can be \
implemented?\n\nFor a different I/O convention where all non-input species \
always start at zero, rather than starting at arbitrary concentrations, how \
does this change what can or cannot be implemented?\n\nHere are some sample \
questions about about programming dynamical behavior in CRNs.  Ditto as \
above.\n\nCan the dual-rail polynomial ODE construction be used to design \
CRNs for steady-state analog computation of algebraic expressions?  And if \
so, how does it compare (e.g. in terms of number of species and reactions, or \
time to compute, or other metrics) to the previously presented constructions? \
 Does it generalize to algebraic expressions that have both positive and \
negative inputs, outputs, and subexpressions?\n\t\nCan the dual-rail \
polynomial ODE construction be generalized to systems of non-polynomial ODEs, \
e.g. via ratios of polynomials, algebraic expressions, or through Taylor \
expansions or other approximations, or other exact methods?  Can you work \
thought a specific chosen example?  Can you assess in general when it will or \
will not work?  \n\t\nFor polynomial ODEs that just so happen to involve \
variables than never go negative, is it possible to derive a simpler \
construction that uses single-rail variables?\n\t\nFor polynomial ODEs that \
have higher-order terms, e.g. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["x", "7"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "63703227-6ef3-40cf-b841-50ca3b839d08"],
 ", the standard dual-rail construction will require a high-order reaction, \
e.g. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"7", " ", "X"}], " ", "\[RightArrow]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"7", "X"}], "+", "Y"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7a074eec-99de-424a-9e6b-64bf8cad999e"],
 ", that might seem difficult to directly implement.  Can such reactions be \
indirectly implemented using a set of several bimolecular reactions?"
}], "Text",
