Cell["\<\


Dynamics in stochastic CRNS.
In the cell nuclues, CRNs are much more dynamic than can be represented by \
most analog CRNs. The R\[ODoubleDot]ssler system is a benchmark for \
deterministic chaos, characterized by extreme sensitivity to initial \
conditions and parameters. In a cell nucleus, however, we do not have a \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]well-mixed beaker.\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] By \
introducing macromolecular crowding (MMC) through trapping and nonconstant \
reaction rates, we are asking whether biological \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]messiness\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] acts as a functional \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]buffer\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] that preserves complex \
dynamics in the face of environmental noise.
 in this approach, I use an implementation of the linear solvation energy \
relationship which is described in assumptions to program a CRN which is \
representative of an approximation to the stochastic dynamics presented by \
the effects of macromolecular crowding within the nucleus. 
Assumptions
CRN dynamics in the cell nucleus is not recapitulated in a well mixed CRN \
model.
\tThis is due to the effects of exclusion volume which follows from \
macromolecular crowding. In the nucleus, the presence of large molecules can \
cause strong and weak \t\teffects, weak effects likely from transient \
hydrophobic interactions and Van Der Waal forces. Strong effects are due to \
mechanisms that tend to kinetically trap molecules such \t\tthat they are \
unable to participate in reactions, this may or may not be correlated to \
their \tdecay mechanisms. In my simulation it is assumed that these are \
independent events \t\tsince trapped molecules can eventually get freed.  \
While this does not violate mass-action kinetics, it introduces \
resonance/damping into the system depending on whether \t\tthe macromolecular \
effects are cooperatively synergetic or not\t\t
Macromolecular crowding effects can be recapitulated via the use of the \
linear solvation energy relationship as developed by Kamlet and Taft \
(Droplets of Life). This is a three parameter polarity scale inclusive of the \
scales of hydrogen bond donor acidity, hydrogen bond acceptor basicity, and \
the polarizability of the molecule. In my system, the rate constant is the \
solute property used in this relationship. The effective rate constant in our \
case is given by 
\tK = K_0 + s*pi + a*alpha + b*beta
\tWhere s, a and b are solute dependent coefficients and pi, alpha, beta \
represent the hydrogen bond donor acidity, hydrogen bond \t\tacceptor \
basicity, and the polarizability of the molecule respectively.
\ts*pi + a*alpha + b*beta is assumed in my model to be linearly dependent on \
K_0 to test the proof of concept since no specific \t\t\tmolecules are \
implied. In this approach, s*pi + a*alpha + b*beta = 1/2(K_0). To simulate \
the stochastic nature, 1/2(K_0) is drawn from a \tPoisson distribution with \
mean 1/2(K_0). Initially, this was done using K_0 + s*pi + a*alpha + b*beta, \
but this lead to significant divergence of our system from the ideal \
solution.
Construction of model:
The \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Memory\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] Effect: By adding \
the X,Y,Z->Xtrapped\:200b, Ytrapped\:200b, Ztrapped\:200b reactions, our \
initial ODE system is now a Delay Differential Equation (DDE) approximation. \
In a standard CRN, the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]now\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
only depends on the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]now.\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] In \
this MMC-CRN, the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]trapped\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
species act as a reservoir for future reactions effectively prolonging the \
reaction, and a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]rate limiting\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
mechanism.
The Poisson Logic: Using Clip[PoissonDistribution[0.5], {-0.5, 0.5}] creates \
a binary switching rate. Your rate constant is either K0-0.5, K0\:200b, K0\
\:200b+0.5 or somewhere in between. This represents a \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]micro-environment\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] model where \
a molecule is either in a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]crowded\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] pocket or a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]free\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] pocket, rather than a smooth Gaussian average.\
\>", "Text",
